If $f(a)=0$, since $f$ is nonnegative and decreasing, $f\equiv 0$ on $[a,b]$.
Thus in this case, there is nothing to do, and it allows to assume that $f(a) > 0$.

Since $f$ is Riemann integrable on $[a,b]$, for fixed $\epsilon>0$ take a partition
$P=\{a=x_0<x_1<...<x_n=b\}$ such that
#eq (eq:B.1:1) {
    \int_a^b f(x)dx < {\epsilon//2} + L(f,P),
}
where $L(f,P)$ is a lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$.
Sicne $f$ is decreasing, one can calcluate $L(f,P)$ explicitly:
$$
    L(f,P) = \sum_{j=1}^n (x_{j}-x_{j-1}) ?(\inf_{x\in[x_{j-1},x_j]}f(x))?
    = \sum_{j=1}^n (x_{j}-x_{j-1})f(x_{j}).
$$

Now we define a nonnegative continuous function $g$ on $[a,b]$ as follows.
Define
$$
    \delta \defeq {\epsilon // f(a)} \land \min_{1<=j<=n}(x_{j} - x_{j-1}) > 0,
$$
define $g(x) = f(b)$ for every $x\in[x_{n-1},b]$,
and construct $g$ on $[x_{j-1},x_j]$ where $j=1,...,n-1$ by
#enum ((\roman*)) {
    \item $g(x)=f(x_j)$ where $x\in[x_{j-1},x_j-\delta]$;
    \item $g$ is defined by a straight line between points $(x_j-\delta,f(x_j))$ and $(x_j,f(x_{j+1}))$.
}
Since $f$ is decreasing, for $j=1,...,n-1$, values of $g$ on $[x_{j-1},x_j]$ is at most $f(x_j)$,
which is clearly less or equal than $f$. In addition, by its construction, $g$ is obviously
nonnegative and continuous. Thus we have $0<=g<=f$, and $g$ is continuous.

It remains to calculate the integral of $g$ on $[a,b]$, which is
useenv align* {
    \int_a^b g(x)dx
    &= \int_{x_{n-1}}^b g(x)dx + \sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\int_{x_{j-1}}^{x_{j}}g(x)dx \\
    &= \int_{x_{n-1}}^b f(b)dx
    + \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} ?(\int_{x_{j-1}}^{x_j - \delta} f(x_j)dx
    + \int_{x_{j}-\delta}^{x_j} {f(x_{j+1}) - f(x_j)//\delta}(x-x_j+\delta) + f(x_j) dx)? \\
    &= f(b)(b-x_{n-1})
    + \sum_{j=1}^{n-1}((x_j - x_{j-1} - \delta) f(x_j) + {\delta//2}(f(x_j) + f(x_{j+1}))).
}
Hence,
$$
    L(f,P) - \int_a^b g(x)dx = {\delta//2}\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}(f(x_{j}) - f(x_{j+1}))
    = {\delta//2}(f(x_1) - f(b)) <= {\delta//2}f(a) <= {\epsilon//2}.
$$
Combining this and \cref{eq:B.1:1}, we obtain the desired conclusion. The proof is now
finished. \proved
